/-!
# Essential smallness of the fibres of an algebraic stack

`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Stacks.EssentiallySmallSite` reduces essential smallness of the
fppf site of an algebraic stack, of its flat-fppf subsite and of its lisse-étale subsite to two
inputs: essential smallness of the size-bounded category of schemes, which is Stacks 000J, and
essential smallness of each fibre `𝒳(U)`, which that file records as "a descent statement about
its smooth presentation and not formal". This file proves the second input, and so makes all
three sites essentially small unconditionally.

The categorical content is a **descent bound**: a stack whose fibres are, locally on the base,
generated by a `w`-small family of objects has `w`-essentially small fibres. Precisely, suppose
that for every object `x` of the fibre over `V` there is a covering sieve of `V` along each of
whose arrows `k : T ⟶ V` the restriction `k^* x` becomes isomorphic to one of a `w`-small family
of objects of the fibre over `T`. Then every fibre has only `w`-many isomorphism classes.

Two measurements shape the proof.

The first is that an isomorphism class in the fibre over `U` is pinned down by a *`w`-small*
amount of data: a covering family of `U`, a member of the given small family over the source of
each of its arrows, and the **transition morphisms** of the identifications over the fibre
products of pairs of arrows of the family. Nothing here needs the total category of the stack to
be small — which it is not, that being what is to be proved — because the transition morphisms
live in morphism sets of the stack, and those are `w`-small by local smallness.

The second is that a covering family may be taken `w`-small even though the covering families the
fppf topology hands out are only bounded by the site's covering cardinal, which lives one
universe up. Every arrow of a covering sieve factors through an arrow out of the chosen
representative of the isomorphism class of its source, so the sieve is already generated by the
arrows it contains whose source is one of those representatives, and there are only `w`-many of
those: that is `CategoryTheory.GrothendieckTopology.ofArrows_skeleton_mem`.

Two objects with the same data are then isomorphic, and *this* is where the stack condition
enters — but only its sheaf half, not effectivity of descent. The two identifications of a given
arrow's restriction with the same member of the small family compose to an isomorphism of the two
restrictions, the equality of transition morphisms says exactly that the two base changes of that
isomorphism to a fibre product agree, and `CategoryTheory.Functor.exists_fiberIso_of_locally` —
the existence half of condition (2) of Stacks 026F, that the presheaves `Mor(x, y)` are sheaves —
glues the resulting compatible family over the covering sieve.

## Main results

* `CategoryTheory.GrothendieckTopology.ofArrows_skeleton_mem`: a covering sieve of an essentially
  small site is generated by the small family of its arrows out of skeleton representatives.
* `CategoryTheory.Functor.fiberCocycle`: the transition morphism of two local identifications of
  one object of a fibre, and
  `CategoryTheory.Functor.baseChangeFiberMor_eq_of_fiberCocycle_eq`, the agreement over the fibre
  product that equal transitions produce.
* `CategoryTheory.Functor.nonempty_iso_of_fiberCocycle_eq`: two objects of a fibre identified with
  the same objects over a covering family, with the same transitions, are isomorphic.
* `CategoryTheory.Functor.small_skeleton_fiber_of_exists_localIso` and
  `CategoryTheory.Functor.essentiallySmall_fiber_of_exists_localIso`: the descent bound.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.essentiallySmall_fiber_algebraicStack`: **the fibres of an algebraic
  stack are essentially small**, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.essentiallySmall_obj_algebraicStack`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.essentiallySmall_flatFppf_algebraicStack`: its fppf site and its
  flat-fppf site are essentially small.

## Implementation notes

No declaration here carries a `@[stacks]` attribute, as none renders a numbered statement of the
source: the source keeps the size-theoretic bookkeeping of Stacks 0787 outside its numbered
statements, and the two reductions this file feeds,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.essentiallySmall_obj` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.essentiallySmall_fullSubsite`, are untagged for the same reason.

The sheaf condition used is the *sieve-indexed* one, condition (2) of Stacks 026F
(`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsStack.isMorSheaf`), which the stack clause of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.IsAlgebraicStack` supplies as its first conjunct. The family-indexed
`Isom` sheaf condition of Stacks 02ZI would serve as well — it is the same condition, by
`CategoryTheory.Functor.isomIsSheafFor_of_isStack` — but it is phrased with arbitrary cartesian
lifts (`CategoryTheory.Functor.Restriction`), whereas the whole argument here is carried out with
the *chosen* pullbacks `CategoryTheory.Functor.pullbackFiber` of the fibred structure, in which
the transition morphisms are plain morphisms of a fibre and the small data above can be written
down.

The three statements for an algebraic stack are instances. The fibre one is what the two
reductions of `EssentiallySmallSite` consume, so it is stated separately from them rather than
folded in, and it is an instance so that a consumer which needs essential smallness of a fibre of
a *variable* algebraic stack gets it without naming this file's theorem. The other two are the
hypotheses that `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Stacks.FlatFppfInclusion` and
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Stacks.FlatFppfRinged` carry as section variables; those files are
upstream of this one, so the variables are discharged at the point of use.

The abstract statements take the small family as a type family `Zs : 𝒞 → Type uz` with a map
`ZZ` into the fibres rather than as a 1-morphism of fibred categories with essentially small
fibres: the geometry of the atlas enters only through the objects it produces in each fibre, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.small_fiber_schemeFibredCategory` is already the smallness of exactly
those objects.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 026F](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/026F)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 026O](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/026O)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0787](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0787)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0788](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0788)
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace CategoryTheory

universe w uz v₁ v₂ u₁ u₂

variable {𝒞 : Type u₁} [Category.{v₁} 𝒞] {𝒳 : Type u₂} [Category.{v₂} 𝒳]

/-- **A covering sieve is generated by its arrows out of skeleton representatives.** Every arrow
of a sieve on `U` factors through the arrow obtained by precomposing it with the isomorphism from
the chosen representative of the isomorphism class of its source, and that arrow is again in the
sieve; so the sieve generated by the arrows of `R` whose source is such a representative is all
of `R`, and in particular covering.

Over an essentially small site the indexing type is `w`-small, which the covering families the
site itself hands out need not be: the covering cardinal of a big fppf site lives in `Type (u+1)`.
-/
theorem GrothendieckTopology.ofArrows_skeleton_mem {J : GrothendieckTopology 𝒞} {U : 𝒞}
    {R : Sieve U} (hR : R ∈ J U) :
    Sieve.ofArrows
      (fun i : {a : Σ c : Skeleton 𝒞, ((fromSkeleton 𝒞).obj c ⟶ U) // R.arrows a.2} =>
        (fromSkeleton 𝒞).obj i.1.1)
      (fun i => i.1.2) ∈ J U := by
  refine J.superset_covering (fun W f hf => ?_) hR
  rw [Sieve.mem_ofArrows_iff]
  exact ⟨⟨⟨toSkeleton W, (fromSkeletonToSkeletonIso W).hom ≫ f⟩, R.downward_closed hf _⟩,
    (fromSkeletonToSkeletonIso W).inv, by simp⟩

namespace Functor

variable (p : 𝒳 ⥤ 𝒞)

/-- The fibres of a functor are locally small as soon as its total category is: the inclusion of
a fibre is faithful. -/
theorem locallySmall_fiber [LocallySmall.{w} 𝒳] (U : 𝒞) : LocallySmall.{w} (p.Fiber U) :=
  ⟨fun _ _ => small_of_injective (f := fun φ => Fiber.fiberInclusion.map φ)
    Faithful.map_injective⟩

/-- **The transition morphism of two local identifications of one object of a fibre.** Let `x` lie
in the fibre over `U`, let `ki : Vi ⟶ U` and `kj : Vj ⟶ U` be two morphisms and let `P` be an
object over both with `pr₁ ≫ ki = pr₂ ≫ kj`. Identifications `αi : ki^* x ≅ Zi` and
`αj : kj^* x ≅ Zj` then transport to a morphism `pr₁^* Zi ⟶ pr₂^* Zj` over `P`, by contracting
the iterated chosen pullbacks of `x` along the two composites, which agree.

For `x` an object descending along a covering family this is the usual cocycle of the descent
datum; `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.essentiallySmall_fiber_algebraicStack` uses it as the part of the
data pinning down an isomorphism class which the objects `Zi` alone do not. -/
noncomputable def fiberCocycle [p.IsFibered] {U Vi Vj P : 𝒞} {ki : Vi ⟶ U} {kj : Vj ⟶ U}
    {pr₁ : P ⟶ Vi} {pr₂ : P ⟶ Vj} (hpr : pr₁ ≫ ki = pr₂ ≫ kj)
    {x : p.Fiber U} {Zi : p.Fiber Vi} {Zj : p.Fiber Vj}
    (αi : p.pullbackFiber x ki ≅ Zi) (αj : p.pullbackFiber x kj ≅ Zj) :
    p.pullbackFiber Zi pr₁ ⟶ p.pullbackFiber Zj pr₂ :=
  (pullbackFiberIso αi pr₁).inv ≫ (pullbackFiberCompIso' x ki pr₁ (pr₁ ≫ ki) rfl).hom ≫
    (pullbackFiberCompIso' x kj pr₂ (pr₁ ≫ ki) hpr.symm).inv ≫ (pullbackFiberIso αj pr₂).hom

variable [p.IsFibered]

/-- **Equal transitions make the two base changes of the comparison agree.** Let `x` and `y` be
objects of the fibre over `U`, each identified over `Vi` and over `Vj` with the same `Zi`
respectively `Zj`. If the two transition morphisms over `P` agree, then the comparisons
`αi ≫ βi⁻¹ : ki^* x ≅ ki^* y` and `αj ≫ βj⁻¹ : kj^* x ≅ kj^* y` have the same base change to `P`.

This is the only computation in the gluing argument: the displayed identity is obtained from the
equality of transitions by cancelling the four identifications. -/
lemma baseChangeFiberMor_eq_of_fiberCocycle_eq {U Vi Vj P : 𝒞} {ki : Vi ⟶ U} {kj : Vj ⟶ U}
    {pr₁ : P ⟶ Vi} {pr₂ : P ⟶ Vj} (hpr : pr₁ ≫ ki = pr₂ ≫ kj)
    {x y : p.Fiber U} {Zi : p.Fiber Vi} {Zj : p.Fiber Vj}
    (αi : p.pullbackFiber x ki ≅ Zi) (αj : p.pullbackFiber x kj ≅ Zj)
    (βi : p.pullbackFiber y ki ≅ Zi) (βj : p.pullbackFiber y kj ≅ Zj)
    (h : fiberCocycle p hpr αi αj = fiberCocycle p hpr βi βj) :
    baseChangeFiberMor (αi ≪≫ βi.symm).hom pr₁ (pr₁ ≫ ki) rfl (pr₁ ≫ ki) rfl =
      baseChangeFiberMor (αj ≪≫ βj.symm).hom pr₂ (pr₁ ≫ ki) hpr.symm (pr₁ ≫ ki) hpr.symm := by
  simp only [fiberCocycle, baseChangeFiberMor, Iso.trans_hom, Iso.symm_hom,
    pullbackFiberHom_comp, ← pullbackFiberIso_hom, ← pullbackFiberIso_inv,
    Category.assoc] at h ⊢
  rw [Iso.inv_comp_eq] at h
  rw [Iso.inv_comp_eq, reassoc_of% h]
  simp

/-- **Two objects of a fibre with the same local data are isomorphic.** Let `p` be a fibred
category over a site whose presheaves `Mor(x, y)` are sheaves — condition (2) of Stacks 026F — and
let `{ k i : V i ⟶ U }` be a covering family. If `x` and `y` in the fibre over `U` are identified
over each `V i` with one and the same object `Z i`, by families whose transition morphisms over
the chosen fibre products `V i ×_U V j` agree, then `x` and `y` are isomorphic.

The composite comparisons `k i ^* x ≅ k i ^* y` agree on the fibre products by
`CategoryTheory.Functor.baseChangeFiberMor_eq_of_fiberCocycle_eq`, hence their base change to an
arrow of the generated sieve does not depend on the factorization of that arrow through the
family, so they assemble into a system over the sieve compatible with base change;
`CategoryTheory.Functor.exists_fiberIso_of_locally` glues it. Only the sheaf condition is used,
not effectivity of descent: nothing is constructed, two given objects are compared. -/
theorem nonempty_iso_of_fiberCocycle_eq [HasPullbacks 𝒞] {J : GrothendieckTopology 𝒞} {U : 𝒞}
    (hmor : ∀ a b : p.Fiber U, p.IsMorSheaf J U a b)
    {I : Type w} {V : I → 𝒞} {k : ∀ i, V i ⟶ U} (hk : Sieve.ofArrows V k ∈ J U)
    {x y : p.Fiber U} {Z : ∀ i, p.Fiber (V i)}
    (α : ∀ i, p.pullbackFiber x (k i) ≅ Z i) (β : ∀ i, p.pullbackFiber y (k i) ≅ Z i)
    (h : ∀ i j, fiberCocycle p (pullback.condition (f := k i) (g := k j)) (α i) (α j) =
      fiberCocycle p (pullback.condition (f := k i) (g := k j)) (β i) (β j)) :
    Nonempty (x ≅ y) := by
  have hindep : ∀ {W : 𝒞} (b : W ⟶ U) (i j : I) (a : W ⟶ V i) (c : W ⟶ V j)
      (ha : a ≫ k i = b) (hc : c ≫ k j = b),
      baseChangeFiberMor (α i ≪≫ (β i).symm).hom a b ha b ha =
        baseChangeFiberMor (α j ≪≫ (β j).symm).hom c b hc b hc := by
    intro W b i j a c ha hc
    have hP : IsPullback (pullback.fst (k i) (k j)) (pullback.snd (k i) (k j)) (k i) (k j) :=
      IsPullback.of_hasPullback (k i) (k j)
    have hac : a ≫ k i = c ≫ k j := by rw [ha, hc]
    have hd1 : hP.lift a c hac ≫ pullback.fst (k i) (k j) = a := hP.lift_fst a c hac
    have hb1 : hP.lift a c hac ≫ (pullback.fst (k i) (k j) ≫ k i) = b := by
      rw [← Category.assoc, hd1, ha]
    calc baseChangeFiberMor (α i ≪≫ (β i).symm).hom a b ha b ha
        = baseChangeFiberMor (baseChangeFiberMor (α i ≪≫ (β i).symm).hom
            (pullback.fst (k i) (k j)) _ rfl _ rfl) (hP.lift a c hac) b hb1 b hb1 := by
          rw [baseChangeFiberMor_baseChangeFiberMor]
          exact baseChangeFiberMor_congr _ hd1.symm _ _ _ _
      _ = baseChangeFiberMor (baseChangeFiberMor (α j ≪≫ (β j).symm).hom
            (pullback.snd (k i) (k j)) _ pullback.condition.symm _ pullback.condition.symm)
            (hP.lift a c hac) b hb1 b hb1 := by
          rw [baseChangeFiberMor_eq_of_fiberCocycle_eq p
            (pullback.condition (f := k i) (g := k j)) (α i) (α j) (β i) (β j) (h i j)]
      _ = baseChangeFiberMor (α j ≪≫ (β j).symm).hom c b hc b hc := by
          rw [baseChangeFiberMor_baseChangeFiberMor]
          exact baseChangeFiberMor_congr _ (hP.lift_snd a c hac) _ _ _ _
  have hex : ∀ (W : 𝒞) (f : W ⟶ U), (Sieve.ofArrows V k).arrows f →
      ∃ (i : I) (a : W ⟶ V i), a ≫ k i = f := fun _ _ hh => exists_factorization_of_ofArrows hh
  choose idx arr harr using hex
  refine ⟨(exists_fiberIso_of_locally p hmor hk
    (fun {_W} f hf => baseChangeFiberIso (α (idx _ f hf) ≪≫ (β (idx _ f hf)).symm)
      (arr _ f hf) f (harr _ f hf) f (harr _ f hf)) ?_).choose⟩
  intro W W' f hf γ b hb hb'
  rw [← baseChangeFiberMor.eq_1]
  simp only [baseChangeFiberIso_hom]
  rw [baseChangeFiberMor_baseChangeFiberMor]
  exact hindep b _ _ _ _ (harr _ b hb') (by rw [Category.assoc, harr _ f hf, hb])

section Code

variable [HasPullbacks 𝒞] {Zs : 𝒞 → Type uz} (ZZ : ∀ V, Zs V → p.Fiber V)

/-- The `w`-small data pinning down an isomorphism class of the fibre over `U`: a set of arrows
into `U` out of skeleton representatives, a member of the small family over the source of each of
them, and a transition morphism over the chosen fibre product of each pair of them. -/
private abbrev FiberCode (U : 𝒞) : Type (max (max (max uz u₁) v₁) v₂) :=
  Σ (s : Set (Σ c : Skeleton 𝒞, ((fromSkeleton 𝒞).obj c ⟶ U)))
    (Z : ∀ a : s, Zs ((fromSkeleton 𝒞).obj a.1.1)),
    ∀ ab : s × s, (p.pullbackFiber (ZZ _ (Z ab.1)) (pullback.fst ab.1.1.2 ab.2.1.2) ⟶
      p.pullbackFiber (ZZ _ (Z ab.2)) (pullback.snd ab.1.1.2 ab.2.1.2))

/-- That a code is the local data of the object `x` of the fibre over `U`: its arrows cover `U`,
and `x` is identified over each of them with the recorded member of the small family, by a family
whose transition morphisms are the recorded ones. -/
private def IsFiberCode (J : GrothendieckTopology 𝒞) {U : 𝒞} (x : p.Fiber U)
    (cd : FiberCode p ZZ U) : Prop :=
  (Sieve.ofArrows (fun i : cd.1 => (fromSkeleton 𝒞).obj i.1.1) (fun i => i.1.2) ∈ J U) ∧
    ∃ α : ∀ i : cd.1, p.pullbackFiber x i.1.2 ≅ ZZ _ (cd.2.1 i),
      ∀ i j, cd.2.2 (i, j) = fiberCocycle p pullback.condition (α i) (α j)

/-- Every object of the fibre over `U` which is locally isomorphic to a member of the small family
has a code: take the arrows of the covering sieve whose source is a skeleton representative, which
still cover by `CategoryTheory.GrothendieckTopology.ofArrows_skeleton_mem`, the members of the
family given over them, and the transition morphisms of the identifications. -/
private theorem exists_isFiberCode {J : GrothendieckTopology 𝒞} {U : 𝒞} (x : p.Fiber U)
    (hZ : ∃ R ∈ J U, ∀ ⦃T : 𝒞⦄ ⦃k : T ⟶ U⦄, R.arrows k →
      ∃ z : Zs T, Nonempty (p.pullbackFiber x k ≅ ZZ T z)) :
    ∃ cd : FiberCode p ZZ U, IsFiberCode p ZZ J x cd := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨R, hR, hlift⟩ := hZ
  choose z hz using
    fun a : {a : Σ c : Skeleton 𝒞, ((fromSkeleton 𝒞).obj c ⟶ U) // R.arrows a.2} => hlift a.2
  exact ⟨⟨{a | R.arrows a.2}, z, fun ab => fiberCocycle p pullback.condition
    (hz ab.1).some (hz ab.2).some⟩,
    GrothendieckTopology.ofArrows_skeleton_mem hR, (fun i => (hz i).some), fun _ _ => rfl⟩

/-- Two objects of the fibre over `U` with one and the same code are isomorphic: their codes
record the same covering family, the same objects over it and the same transitions, which is the
hypothesis of `CategoryTheory.Functor.nonempty_iso_of_fiberCocycle_eq`. -/
private theorem nonempty_iso_of_isFiberCode {J : GrothendieckTopology 𝒞} {U : 𝒞}
    (hmor : ∀ a b : p.Fiber U, p.IsMorSheaf J U a b) {x y : p.Fiber U} {cd : FiberCode p ZZ U}
    (hx : IsFiberCode p ZZ J x cd) (hy : IsFiberCode p ZZ J y cd) : Nonempty (x ≅ y) := by
  obtain ⟨hcov, α, hα⟩ := hx
  obtain ⟨-, β, hβ⟩ := hy
  exact nonempty_iso_of_fiberCocycle_eq p hmor hcov α β fun i j => by rw [← hα i j, ← hβ i j]

/-- **The descent bound, on isomorphism classes.** Let `p : 𝒳 ⥤ 𝒞` be a fibred category over an
essentially small, locally small site whose presheaves `Mor(x, y)` are sheaves, with `𝒳` locally
small, and let `ZZ` pick out, in each fibre, the objects of a `w`-small family. If every object of
every fibre becomes, along every arrow of some covering sieve, isomorphic to a member of the
family, then every fibre has only `w`-many isomorphism classes.

The isomorphism class of an object is sent to the code of
`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsFiberCode`, which is a `w`-small amount of data, and two objects with
the same code are isomorphic. -/
theorem small_skeleton_fiber_of_exists_localIso [Small.{w} (Skeleton 𝒞)]
    [LocallySmall.{w} 𝒞] [LocallySmall.{w} 𝒳] [∀ V, Small.{w} (Zs V)]
    {J : GrothendieckTopology 𝒞} (hmor : ∀ (V : 𝒞) (a b : p.Fiber V), p.IsMorSheaf J V a b)
    (hZ : ∀ (V : 𝒞) (x : p.Fiber V), ∃ R ∈ J V, ∀ ⦃T : 𝒞⦄ ⦃k : T ⟶ V⦄, R.arrows k →
      ∃ z : Zs T, Nonempty (p.pullbackFiber x k ≅ ZZ T z))
    (U : 𝒞) : Small.{w} (Skeleton (p.Fiber U)) := by
  have hls : ∀ V : 𝒞, LocallySmall.{w} (p.Fiber V) := fun V => locallySmall_fiber p V
  choose Φ hΦ using fun Q : Skeleton (p.Fiber U) =>
    exists_isFiberCode p ZZ ((fromSkeleton (p.Fiber U)).obj Q) (hZ U _)
  refine small_of_injective (f := Φ) fun Q₁ Q₂ hEq => ?_
  obtain ⟨e⟩ := nonempty_iso_of_isFiberCode p ZZ (hmor U) (hΦ Q₁) (hEq ▸ hΦ Q₂)
  rw [← toSkeleton_fromSkeleton_obj Q₁, ← toSkeleton_fromSkeleton_obj Q₂]
  exact congr_toSkeleton_of_iso e

/-- **The descent bound.** Under the hypotheses of
`CategoryTheory.Functor.small_skeleton_fiber_of_exists_localIso`, every fibre of `p` is
essentially small: its isomorphism classes are `w`-small, and it is locally small because `𝒳`
is. -/
theorem essentiallySmall_fiber_of_exists_localIso [Small.{w} (Skeleton 𝒞)]
    [LocallySmall.{w} 𝒞] [LocallySmall.{w} 𝒳] [∀ V, Small.{w} (Zs V)]
    {J : GrothendieckTopology 𝒞} (hmor : ∀ (V : 𝒞) (a b : p.Fiber V), p.IsMorSheaf J V a b)
    (hZ : ∀ (V : 𝒞) (x : p.Fiber V), ∃ R ∈ J V, ∀ ⦃T : 𝒞⦄ ⦃k : T ⟶ V⦄, R.arrows k →
      ∃ z : Zs T, Nonempty (p.pullbackFiber x k ≅ ZZ T z))
    (U : 𝒞) : EssentiallySmall.{w} (p.Fiber U) :=
  (essentiallySmall_iff (p.Fiber U)).2
    ⟨small_skeleton_fiber_of_exists_localIso p ZZ hmor hZ U, locallySmall_fiber p U⟩

end Code

end Functor

end CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Stack

open CategoryTheory

universe u w

variable (B : BigFppfSite.{u}) (S : Scheme.{u}) (hS : B.Contains S)

/-- **The fibres of an algebraic stack are essentially small.** The categories of scheme-valued
points of an algebraic stack over `S` have only `u`-many isomorphism classes, `u` being the
universe of the schemes of the site.

This is the remaining, non-formal input of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Stacks.EssentiallySmallSite`. The scheme presentation of
Stacks 026O is locally essentially surjective
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.exists_atlas_localEssSurj`), so every object of every fibre becomes,
along every arrow of a covering sieve, isomorphic to the image of an object of the fibre of the
presenting scheme's category, and those fibres are small
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.small_fiber_schemeFibredCategory`); the descent bound
`CategoryTheory.Functor.essentiallySmall_fiber_of_exists_localIso` then applies, with the sheaf
condition taken from the first conjunct of the stack clause. -/
instance essentiallySmall_fiber_algebraicStack (𝒳 : AlgebraicStacks.{u, w} B S hS)
    (U : BigFppfOver B S hS) : EssentiallySmall.{u} (𝒳.val.val.p.Fiber U) := by
  have := 𝒳.val.isFiberedInGroupoids.isFibered
  have := essentiallySmall_bigFppfOver B S hS
  obtain ⟨U₀, f, -, hsurj⟩ := exists_atlas_localEssSurj B S hS 𝒳
  obtain ⟨-, ⟨hst, -⟩, -⟩ := 𝒳.property
  have : ∀ V, Small.{u} ((schemeFibredCategory B S hS U₀).val.p.Fiber V) :=
    small_fiber_schemeFibredCategory B S hS U₀
  refine Functor.essentiallySmall_fiber_of_exists_localIso 𝒳.val.val.p
    (Zs := fun V => (schemeFibredCategory B S hS U₀).val.p.Fiber V)
    (fun _ z => Functor.Fiber.mk ((Functor.congr_obj f.hom.w z.1).trans z.2))
    (fun V a b => hst.isMorSheaf V a b) ?_ U
  intro V x
  obtain ⟨R, hR, hlift⟩ := hsurj V x
  refine ⟨R, hR, fun {T} {k} hk => ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨z, φ, hφ⟩ := hlift hk
  have hzb : 𝒳.val.val.p.obj ((CatFiberedInGroupoids.homFunctor f).obj z) = T :=
    IsHomLift.domain_eq 𝒳.val.val.p k φ
  exact ⟨Functor.Fiber.mk ((Functor.congr_obj f.hom.w z).symm.trans hzb),
    ⟨(Functor.Restriction.ofCartesian hzb x.2 φ
      (𝒳.val.isFiberedInGroupoids.isStronglyCartesian_homLift k φ)).fiberIso⟩⟩

/-- **The fppf site of an algebraic stack is essentially small**, which is what makes
sheafification available on it and hence its topos, its structure sheaf and its module categories
exist. This is `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.essentiallySmall_obj` with its fibre hypothesis
discharged. -/
instance essentiallySmall_obj_algebraicStack (𝒳 : AlgebraicStacks.{u, w} B S hS) :
    EssentiallySmall.{u} 𝒳.val.val.obj :=
  essentiallySmall_obj B S hS 𝒳.val fun U => essentiallySmall_fiber_algebraicStack B S hS 𝒳 U

/-- **The flat-fppf site of an algebraic stack is essentially small**: it is a full subcategory of
the fppf site. This is `AlgebraicGeometry.Stack.essentiallySmall_fullSubsite` at the flat objects,
with its hypothesis discharged. -/
instance essentiallySmall_flatFppf_algebraicStack (𝒳 : AlgebraicStacks.{u, w} B S hS) :
    EssentiallySmall.{u} (FlatFppf B S hS 𝒳) :=
  essentiallySmall_fullSubsite B S hS 𝒳.val (flatObject B S hS 𝒳.val)

end AlgebraicGeometry.Stack

end
